    "body": "EICMA 2026 • Triumph TF-E Bikes Get Even More Enduro With Updates for the 2027 Model Year\nJeremy Clarkson's Tesla Cybercab Experience Reveals Flaws That Need To Be Addressed\n14 photos\nPhoto: Tesla\nAn autonomous taxi should have one simple job beyond getting its passenger to the desired destination, and that is to stop when the passenger asks it to. The Cybercab apparently struggled with that part of the assignment when Jeremy Clarkson of Top Gear and Grand Tour experienced the driverless Tesla.\nHis Austin experience involved a failed pickup, a Cybercab that ignored Clarkson's inputted destination, and a particularly agonizing period when Jeremy could not persuade the car to stop or even open its doors. Had it been a medical emergency, this kind of shortfall would have been completely unacceptable.\nJC recounted the Cybercab experience in his latest column for The Sunday Times, offering a less enthusiastic account of Tesla's design and self-driving software than what you might have seen from social media influencers who like to praise Elon Musk for just about everything, including the bad parts of his personality.\nGetting inside the Cybercab proved troublesome for Clarkson. He watched the vehicle approach his pickup location through the app, only for the Cybercab in question to stop around the corner. Imagine this error happening during a torrential downpour. After climbing aboard, the Cybercab remained stationary while its digital assistant insisted that it was already heading toward the destination.\nAs fate would have it, a human representative eventually intervened, speaking through the car's audio system. The Tesla representative explained to Clarkson that he must cancel the booking and summon another vehicle, adding to his frustration. The replacement Cybercab navigated traffic smoothly enough, and JC humorously tested its conversational abilities during the ride by talking about the Tottenham Hotspur football club.\nPhoto: Tesla The replacement Cybercab, however, fumbled Clarkson once it had reached the destination. Not only did it continue driving, but the auto-driving vehicle announced that it needed to find somewhere else safe enough to pull over. Jeremy claims the car had already passed several places that looked perfectly suitable.\nDuring this process, Clarkson noticed a few glitches in the central display that represented surrounding traffic. Phantom braking and an awkward encounter with an autonomous shopping trolley are further mentioned, and worse still, the car \"seemingly\" went through some red lights. \"Seemingly\" does the heavy lifting here, but nevertheless, running a red light is unacceptable auto-driving behavior.\nLast but not least, Clarkson's account includes multiple ignored requests to stop and open the doors. JC admitted that said experience unsettled him, acknowledging that he could not do anything meaningful to resolve the aforementioned issues. The problem here is that Tesla removes the steering wheel and the pedal box, a perfectly understandable decision for a machine that never expects a human to take over.\nManual controls are welcomed in situations that cannot be resolved by software alone. As it stands, the Cybercab passenger depends on the vehicle's software recognizing the problem and accepting the instruction to stop. Given the British lad's experience, how are you guys feeling about Tesla intending to launch customer deliveries before year's end?\nIf you ask me, that would be a big fat no. As long as the Cybercab runs red lights, imagines things in front of it, and overlooks a passenger's instruction to stop, the software definitely needs polishing.\nVia // The Times\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\njeremy clarkson tesla cybercab Tesla EV self-driving autonomous vehicle robotaxi\nAbout the author: Mircea Panait\nAfter a 1:43 scale model of a Ferrari 250 GTO sparked Mircea's interest for cars when he was a kid, an early internship at Top Gear sealed his career path. He's most interested in muscle cars and American trucks, but he takes a passing interest in quirky kei cars as well. Full profile\nUP NEXT Car Nut Gets Tipped on Survivor 1969 Super Bee 4-Speed, Runs to It, Cash in Hand, Finds Big Surprise",
